      ],
      "text": "Resistivity depends on temperature — usually in metals",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0002",
      "normalized_math": "R \\uparrow \\quad T \\uparrow",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0002"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "R increases as T increases",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0003",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0003"
      ],
      "text": "(Superconductors — materials with zero resistance — are generally extremely low temperature. “High temperature” superconductors are those which exhibit R = 0 above 77 K, the boiling point of nitrogen.)",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0004",
      "level": 1,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0004"
      ],
      "text": "Ohmic Materials (Every material in 1B)",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0005",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0005"
      ],
      "text": "Ohmic materials obey Ohm’s Law:",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0006",
      "normalized_math": "I=\\frac{V}{R}",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0008"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "I equals V divided by R",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "complex",
      "detailed_figure_description": "An I-versus-V coordinate graph has current I on the vertical axis and voltage V on the horizontal axis. Two blue straight lines pass through the origin with different positive slopes. Above the graph is the relation I = (1/R)V. A blue note to the right says that as R increases, delta I over delta V decreases.",
      "figure_alternative": "I-versus-V graph for ohmic materials, showing straight lines through the origin and a smaller slope for greater resistance.",
      "id": "page-4-s0007",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0006"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0008",
      "normalized_math": "I=\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\right)V",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0007"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "I equals one over R times V",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0009",
      "normalized_math": "R\\uparrow \\quad \\frac{\\Delta I}{\\Delta V}\\downarrow",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0009"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "As R increases, delta I over delta V decreases",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0010",
      "level": 2,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0010"
      ],
      "text": "Ohm’s Law: V = IR",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0011",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0011"
      ],
      "text": "Example In the circuit below, is V higher at point A or B? What about I?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "complex",
      "detailed_figure_description": "A single-loop circuit contains a resistor in the top wire between labeled points A on the left and B on the right. An orange arrow above the resistor points from A toward B and is labeled I. A battery is in the bottom wire, with its positive terminal on the left and negative terminal on the right.",
      "figure_alternative": "Series circuit with current flowing from A through a resistor to B; the battery is positive on the left and negative on the right.",
      "id": "page-4-s0012",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0012"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0013",
      "normalized_math": "V=IR,\\quad \\text{so}\\quad V_A>V_B.",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0013"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "V equals I R, so V sub A is greater than V sub B",
      "type": "formula"
